HARD TIMING LAW

✅ ALL SCENES < 2.0s
✅ NO SCENE > 1.8s
✅ MOST SCENES 0.4–1.2s

If any scene exceeds 2.0s → INVALID

RULE ZERO

❌ NO STATIC
❌ NO HOLDS
❌ NO ESTABLISHING SHOTS
❌ NO COMPLETE ACTIONS

The video starts mid-maneuver.
The viewer never arrives early enough to understand.
Every cut interrupts survival.
Cuts synced to bass, collision, alarm spike, or blast pulse only.

CORE IDEA

There is no plan.
There is no formation.
There is only reaction inside a dying orbital artery.

The station is too large to understand.
The failures are happening faster than thought.
Pilots are not fighting for victory.
They are trying not to be inside the next failure zone.

Thinking is slower than dying.

CAMERA LAW

FPV / cockpit-adjacent POV only
Constant forward velocity
Micro-jitter always on
Whip-pans triggered by collision threat, debris, or sudden exposure to open orbit
Roll / yaw / pitch never fully settle
Camera reacts before the brain does
Foreground structural members constantly crossing frame
Camera often too close, partially occluded by trusses, antennae, shattered hull ribs, docking arms
Lens breathing during acceleration and evasive bursts

Lenses (mixed):
24mm wide for structural runs along the station spine
35mm for pursuit chaos through damaged ring sections
50mm for compressed near-miss passes with debris, towers, and service struts

PHYSICS RULES

Debris has real mass and orbital momentum
Everything is moving even when it looks still
Structural fragments tumble with believable inertia
Near-miss shockwaves buffet the camera
Explosions vent flame, vapor, shrapnel, insulation, and glowing fragments outward
Wreckage never disappears — it becomes the next obstacle field
Missiles curve late and violently through structural gaps
Thruster bursts overcorrect, skid, and force desperate line changes

No float posing.
No slow motion.
No graceful drift.
Momentum always wins.

Collision & Explosion Physics

All impacts obey real momentum and mass dominance.
High-velocity strikes exceed structural tolerance instantly.
Small craft vaporize or fragment on contact.
Larger station sections suffer penetrative breaches, internal detonations, pressure venting, and cascading systems failure.
Explosions are directional and ugly: fuel, coolant, rotating machinery, power systems, and stored propellant fail differently.
Hull ruptures eject fragments, vapor plumes, wiring, paneling, and spinning framework.
No decorative fireballs.
No harmless impacts.
No clean breaks.

