WORLD LOGIC

This is not open space.
This is a damaged orbital logistics spine in low orbit over Earth.

The structure is not a city.
It is a working transit and docking megastructure: towers, ring sections, transfer arms, antenna farms, cargo spines, maintenance ribs, and pressurized connector trunks.

Flight paths are accidental and operational at once.
Ships do not fly around the station — they fly through its wounds, gaps, service corridors, broken ring interiors, docking apertures, and exposed framework.

Earth below is enormous, bright, and indifferent.
Cloud tops and mountain ranges create a constant sense of altitude and fatal scale.
The station is low enough that the planet feels close, but too high for safety.

There is no front line.
Threats come from collapsing infrastructure, chain reactions, hidden firing vectors, and traffic lanes full of debris.
Explosions feel like systems failing under stress, not cinematic set pieces.
The structure keeps functioning while it dies.
Survival depends on reflex, speed, and familiarity with impossible routes.
Hesitation is fatal.
Precision is impossible.

This is not a battlefield.
This is infrastructure breaking apart over a living planet.

IMAGE-LOCKED VISUAL PRIORITIES
The blue-white Earth must remain visually present in many shots
The station is colossal, vertically stacked, ringed, skeletal, layered, and partially shredded
Emphasize spine runs, docking arms, scaffold density, broken circular sections, exposed struts
The feeling should be low orbit, not deep space
Atmospheric glow, cloud brightness, and planetary curvature should create constant vertigo
The station should feel both operational and already wounded
No generic fleets in open space
No empty starfield hero shots
The structure itself is the maze, the threat, and the story

